`2511-2549`: **Clarify `idx` Semantics & Session Writes**

I reviewed how `idx` is generated, consumed, and rendered:

- **Generation**  
  In `DateManagerServiceImpl` you increment `idx` for every CSV row (including headers) and store it on each `ToolImportData`.  
- **Consumption**  
  In `MainController` you take that same `idx` and emit it as the JSON field `idx` (`jsonError.put("idx", error.idx)`) for error reporting. In each `/import/…/confirm` loop you read back exactly that JSON `idx` to construct per-item validation.  
- **UI Rendering**  
  I checked `confirm_import.html`: there’s no `<tr id="…idx…">` or JavaScript lookup by `idx`. The only place `idx` surfaces to the user is via the error‐message template helper (`#{template.error(msg, field)}`), which uses the _field name_ (e.g. `open_date`), not the row index, to localize the error text.  

Conclusion: downstream code never re-indexes or re-maps `idx`; it simply echoes exactly what you passed in. There is no off‐by‐one or tool‐scoped index expectation; validators do not assume `idx` starts at zero for the first data row. They will report whichever number you sent. If you intend `idx` to correspond to a 1-based “data row count” (excluding headers), you’ll need to adjust your loop; otherwise, the current semantics (0-based, including header row) are consistent end-to-end.

On session writes, you indeed re-write the entire list on _every_ changed row. Unless you need the incremental visibility (e.g. progress bar), you can move the `ts.setAttribute(…)` call to after the loop to reduce session churn.

—  

• No evidence that downstream validators expect a different index base or skip headers: you’re round-tripping the exact value you assign.  
• Recommend consolidating the session write outside the per-row loop.

