`124-135`: **Cache the owner `Field` lookup**

We still do a fresh `ReflectionUtils.findField(...)` and `makeAccessible(...)` on every invocation. Resolve it once and reuse a cached `Field` so site creation/update avoids repeated reflective scans (same concern noted earlier). You can memoise per concrete class to keep it flexible for proxies:

```diff
+import java.util.concurrent.ConcurrentHashMap;
+
+    private static final Map<Class<?>, Field> CREATED_USER_ID_FIELDS = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
+
     private void setCreatedUserId(Site site, String ownerId) {
-        Field field = ReflectionUtils.findField(site.getClass(), "m_createdUserId");
-        if (field == null) {
-            throw new IllegalStateException("Unable to locate the site owner field");
-        }
-        ReflectionUtils.makeAccessible(field);
+        Field field = CREATED_USER_ID_FIELDS.computeIfAbsent(site.getClass(), clazz -> {
+            Field f = ReflectionUtils.findField(clazz, "m_createdUserId");
+            if (f == null) {
+                throw new IllegalStateException("Unable to locate the site owner field on " + clazz.getName());
+            }
+            ReflectionUtils.makeAccessible(f);
+            return f;
+        });
         try {
             ReflectionUtils.setField(field, site, ownerId);
         } catch (IllegalArgumentException e) {
             throw new IllegalStateException("Unable to set the site owner field", e);
         }
     }
```

