**Set HTTP status code and content type in `doError` to ensure error responses are properly signaled to LTI tool consumers**

The `doError` method now centralizes error XML generation via `MessageResponseBuilder`, but does not set HTTP status codes or content type. Currently, error responses are returned with the default HTTP 200 status, which may confuse LTI tool consumers expecting error status codes.

The codebase shows inconsistency: early guard-clause errors (lines 185, 640) set `SC_FORBIDDEN` directly and return, while business logic errors call `doError` without setting status. To maintain consistency and ensure clients properly recognize errors at the HTTP layer, add status code and content type to `doError`:

```java
response.setStatus(HttpServletResponse.SC_BAD_REQUEST);
response.setContentType("application/xml");
response.setCharacterEncoding("UTF-8");
```

Alternatively, if different error types warrant different status codes (e.g., `SC_INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR` for exception-based errors vs. `SC_BAD_REQUEST` for validation), move status setting to call sites where the error context is known.

</blockquote></details>

