<details>
<summary>gradebookng/tool/src/java/org/sakaiproject/gradebookng/tool/model/GbGradebookData.java (1)</summary><blockquote>

`694-719`: **Score.getScore() NPE risk and over-complicated isPackable logic (same issue as earlier review)**

This block still has the two issues previously flagged in past reviews:

- `getScore()` returns a primitive `double` but attempts to return `null` when `this.score` is null, which will cause a `NullPointerException` due to unboxing.
- `isPackable()` has redundant grade-type checks and repeats `Double.valueOf(this.score)` unnecessarily.

This is a functional bug plus unnecessary complexity.





A more robust implementation would be:

```diff
-        // We assume you'll check isNull() prior to calling this
-        public double getScore() {
-            return this.score == null ? null : Double.valueOf(this.score);
-        };
+        // We assume you'll check isNull() prior to calling this
+        public Double getScore() {
+            return this.score == null ? null : Double.valueOf(this.score);
+        }
@@
-        public boolean isPackable() {
-            return settings.getGradeType() != GradeType.LETTER && (isNull() || settings.getGradeType() == GradeType.LETTER || (Double.valueOf(this.score) >= 0 && Double.valueOf(this.score) < 16384));
-        }
+        public boolean isPackable() {
+            // Letter grades are never packed; they always go via JSON.
+            if (settings.getGradeType() == GradeType.LETTER) {
+                return false;
+            }
+            // Null scores can be packed using the sentinel.
+            if (isNull()) {
+                return true;
+            }
+            Double value = getScore();
+            if (value == null) {
+                return false;
+            }
+            return value >= 0 && value < 16384;
+        }
```

After changing the return type of `getScore()`, please recompile and update any external callers to expect a nullable `Double`.


```shell
#!/bin/bash
# Find all usages of Score.getScore() to ensure they are compatible with the new Double return type
rg -n "getScore\(" .
```

