`325-337`: **`getDeclaredFields()` doesn't walk the class hierarchy — fine today but fragile for future subclassing.**

`getFieldsForClass` only scans `c.getDeclaredFields()`, which returns fields declared in that specific class — not inherited fields. Currently all beans directly extend `FoormBaseBean`, so this works. If a bean subclass ever extends another bean (e.g., `SpecialContentBean extends LtiContentBean`), its parent's `[user]` fields would silently be missing from the cache.

Consider walking the superclass chain up to `FoormBaseBean` to future-proof:


<details>
<summary>♻️ Walk the class hierarchy to collect all FoormField annotations</summary>

```diff
     protected static Map<String, Field> getFieldsForClass(Class<?> clazz) {
         return FIELDS_CACHE.computeIfAbsent(clazz, c -> {
             Map<String, Field> map = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
-            for (Field f : c.getDeclaredFields()) {
-                FoormField ann = f.getAnnotation(FoormField.class);
-                if (ann != null) {
-                    f.setAccessible(true);
-                    map.put(ann.value(), f);
+            Class<?> current = c;
+            while (current != null && current != FoormBaseBean.class) {
+                for (Field f : current.getDeclaredFields()) {
+                    FoormField ann = f.getAnnotation(FoormField.class);
+                    if (ann != null && !map.containsKey(ann.value())) {
+                        f.setAccessible(true);
+                        map.put(ann.value(), f);
+                    }
                 }
+                current = current.getSuperclass();
             }
             return map;
         });
```
</details>

---

`382-407`: **`toArchiveElement` iterates `getDeclaredFields()` directly instead of using the cached field map.**

This duplicates the reflection walk and is subject to the same hierarchy limitation noted above. Consider reusing `getFieldsForClass(getClass())` for consistency:


<details>
<summary>♻️ Reuse the cached field map</summary>

```diff
     public Element toArchiveElement(Document doc, String tagName) {
         if (doc == null) {
             return null;
         }
         Set<String> excluded = getExcludedArchiveFieldNames();
         Element root = doc.createElement(tagName);
-        for (Field f : getClass().getDeclaredFields()) {
-            FoormField ann = f.getAnnotation(FoormField.class);
-            if (ann == null || !ann.archive() || excluded.contains(ann.value())) {
+        Map<String, Field> fields = getFieldsForClass(getClass());
+        for (Map.Entry<String, Field> entry : fields.entrySet()) {
+            Field f = entry.getValue();
+            FoormField ann = f.getAnnotation(FoormField.class);
+            if (ann == null || !ann.archive() || excluded.contains(entry.getKey())) {
                 continue;
             }
-            String field = ann.value();
-            Object o = getValueByFieldName(field);
+            Object o = getValueByFieldName(entry.getKey());
```
</details>

---

`438-473`: **`populateFromArchiveElement` does not check `getExcludedArchiveFieldNames()`.**

`toArchiveElement` respects the excluded set, but `populateFromArchiveElement` does not. This asymmetry means that if archive XML from an external source contains an excluded field (e.g., a checksum appended separately), it would still be populated onto the bean. This may be intentional (e.g., you *want* to read back the checksum during merge), but worth documenting or aligning if not.

